        <div class="pf-who-item pf-who-yes"><span class="pf-who-icon">1</span><div><strong>Find the stopcock and turn it off</strong><p>For any water leak, the stopcock is your first move. In most Aigburth Victorian terraces it sits under the kitchen sink or in a hallway cupboard. In Edwardian semis it's often in a utility room or near the front door. Turn it clockwise. That stops all water until we arrive.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-who-item pf-who-yes"><span class="pf-who-icon">2</span><div><strong>If you smell gas, get out</strong><p>Don't touch any switches. Open windows and doors as you leave. Call <a href="https://cadentgas.com/gas-escapes" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--orange);font-weight:700;">Cadent Gas on 0800 111 999</a> first. Then call us on 0151 558 0334 once the property's safe.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-who-item pf-who-yes"><span class="pf-who-icon">3</span><div><strong>No heating? Check the pressure gauge</strong><p>Look at the gauge on the boiler. It should sit between 1 and 1.5 bar. Below 0.5, you can repressurise using the filling loop underneath. That fixes about half of all "no heating" calls. If it doesn't work, call us.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-who-item pf-who-yes"><span class="pf-who-icon">4</span><div><strong>Photograph the damage</strong><p>Take photos and video before you start cleaning. Water damage claims need evidence. We write our own report with photos as well, ready for your insurer. We know what the big insurers need.</p></div></div>
